= Solution
{parent=i}

A <stationary point> occurs where the <derivative> vanishes. Here
$$
f'(x)=6+12\sin(3x)=0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\sin(3x)=-\frac12.
$$
The two families are
$$
\boxed{x=\frac{7\pi}{18}+\frac{2n\pi}{3}\quad\text{or}\quad x=\frac{11\pi}{18}+\frac{2n\pi}{3},\qquad n\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
Consequently there are **infinitely many** <stationary points> on the real line. The second <derivative> is $36\cos(3x)$: the first family consists of strict <local minima> and the second of strict <local maxima>.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <product rule> and <chain rule> give
$$
f'(x)=10e^{3-x^2}(1-2x^2).
$$
The <exponential function> is strictly positive, so there are exactly **two** <stationary points>, at $\boxed{x=\pm1/\sqrt2}$. The <derivative> is negative outside these two values and positive between them. Thus the negative value is a strict <local minimum> and the positive value a strict <local maximum>.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

By the <product rule> and <chain rule>,
$$
f'(x)=e^{x^2}(1+2x^2)>0\qquad(x\in\mathbb R).
$$
Thus there are $\boxed{0}$ <stationary points>; the function is strictly increasing everywhere.

