= Solution
{parent=3}

The <arc length> of the <helix> follows from its parametrization:
$$
\mathbf r'(t)=\bigl(-10\sin(t-\pi/2),\ 10\cos(t-\pi/2),\ 5\bigr),\qquad
|\mathbf r'(t)|=\sqrt{100+25}=5\sqrt5.
$$
Thus $\ell_h=15\pi\sqrt5\,\mathrm m$. Its endpoint is $(0,20,15\pi)$, at height $h=15\pi\,\mathrm m$.

Under the usual intended interpretation that the descending quarter of a <circle> drops through one <radius>, its <radius> is $R=h$. Its <arc length> is $\ell_c=\pi R/2=15\pi^2/2\,\mathrm m$, giving
$$
\boxed{\ell_h+\ell_c=15\pi\sqrt5+\frac{15\pi^2}{2}=179.39425397\ldots\,\mathrm m\simeq179\,\mathrm m.}
$$
**The printed smoothness condition is inconsistent.** The terminal <tangent vector> of the <helix> is $\mathbf r'(3\pi)=(-10,0,5)$. Every <tangent vector> to a <curve> confined to the $yz$ <plane> has zero $x$ component, so no such <regular curve> can join this <helix> with a common <tangent line>. Moreover, the <helix> is still rising at that endpoint. Thus no smooth ride satisfying all the literal conditions exists.

Even after allowing a corner, a quarter of a <circle> reaching the ground does not alone determine its <radius>: the orientation of its endpoint radii must also be specified. For example, in the $yz$ <plane>, the circular arc
$$
y=20+R\bigl(\cos\theta-\cos\theta_0\bigr),\quad
z=h+R\bigl(\sin\theta-\sin\theta_0\bigr),\quad
\theta_0-\pi/2\le\theta\le\theta_0,
$$
traversed toward decreasing $\theta$, descends monotonically for $0\le\theta_0\le\pi/2$ and reaches $z=0$ when $R=h/(\sin\theta_0+\cos\theta_0)$. Choosing $\theta_0=\pi/4$ gives $R=h/\sqrt2$, whereas $\theta_0=\pi/2$ gives $R=h$. The numerical answer above is conditional on the intended $R=h$ geometry; it does not repair the impossible smooth join.

= 4
{parent=2021}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

At their intersection, $x^2+y^2=1$ and $z^2=1$. The <gradients> of the defining functions give <normal vectors> to the <sphere> and <cylinder>:
$$
\mathbf n_s=(2x,2y,2z),\qquad \mathbf n_c=(2x,2y,0).
$$
The acute angle between the <tangent planes> is the acute angle between these <normal vectors>. Their <dot product> gives
$$
\cos\alpha=\frac{|\mathbf n_s\cdot\mathbf n_c|}{|\mathbf n_s|\,|\mathbf n_c|}
=\frac4{(2\sqrt2)(2)}=\frac1{\sqrt2},\qquad
\boxed{\alpha=\frac\pi4=45^\circ.}
$$
It is the same at every intersection point; the supplementary oriented angle is $135^\circ$.

= 5
{parent=2021}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Interpret the cube root as the real cube root. The curved boundary is $y=-x^3$, so the bounded region is $0\le x\le3$, $-x^3\le y\le0$. In particular $y$ is negative inside it. The <double integral> is the convergent <improper integral>
$$
I=\int_0^3\frac1{x^3+1}\left(\int_{-x^3}^0y^{-1/3}\,dy\right)dx.
$$
For negative real $y$, an <antiderivative> is $(3/2)y^{2/3}=(3/2)|y|^{2/3}$. The inner <integral> is therefore $-3x^2/2$. Hence
$$
I=-\frac32\int_0^3\frac{x^2}{x^3+1}\,dx
=-\frac12\bigl[\ln(x^3+1)\bigr]_0^3,
\qquad \boxed{I=-\frac12\ln28.}
$$
The negative sign is essential. Absolute convergence follows by the same calculation with the sign removed, so the singular edge at $y=0$ causes no ambiguity.

= 6
{parent=2021}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Parametrize the <curve> by $\mathbf r(x)=(x,e^x,e^x)$ for $0\le x\le1$. Then $d\mathbf r=(1,e^x,e^x)\,dx$ and $\mathbf F(\mathbf r(x))=(e^{2x},x,e^x)$. Their <dot product> gives the <line integral>
$$
\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=\int_0^1\left(2e^{2x}+xe^x\right)dx.
$$
Using <integration by parts> for the second term,
$$
\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
=\bigl[e^{2x}+(x-1)e^x\bigr]_0^1,
\qquad\boxed{\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=e^2.}
$$

